<h1>Web accessibility audit services</h1>
<p class="lede">Four ways to work with tuaccesible, depending on what you already know about your product and how much time you have.</p>

<p>María Pía runs the manual testing. She has used a screen reader as her primary interface for 13 years. That changes what gets tested: she works through a form the way someone who depends on that technology does, not the way someone who switches a screen reader on to spot-check it. And no report ships after one pass: a second person reviews the findings and their priority before they reach you. See <a href="/en/team/">who audits your site</a>.</p>

<p><strong>A quote is free, and there's nothing to sign.</strong> Send us the address of the site, roughly how many distinct templates it has, which journeys need reviewing and whether there's a logged-in area. Within a week you get scope, price and timeline in writing, before anything starts. There's no public price list, because no two sites are alike.</p>
<p>The quote tells you what it costs and how long it takes. It doesn't include the list of what's wrong: that's the Express audit, which also sharpens the quote for the bigger job, because by then we know what we're dealing with.</p>

<p><strong>Not sure which one you need?</strong> If you need an answer this week, start with the Express audit. If you need a document that stands up to a regulator or a client, that's the Site audit. If you also need the fixes to actually get made, take the Full package. If the problem is that your team keeps rebuilding the same barriers, book the Corporate workshop.</p>

<div class="prose">
  <h2>Working with us from the United States</h2>
  <p>tuaccesible is based in Argentina and works with clients in the United States, Spain and Latin America. Three things are worth saying before you ask.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Hours.</strong> We're on UTC-3, one to two hours ahead of US Eastern time. A 10am call in New York is early afternoon here, and our working day overlaps almost entirely with yours. There's no overnight handoff and no waiting a day for an answer.</li>
    <li><strong>Language.</strong> Every written deliverable is produced in English: the report, the criteria table, the accessibility statement and the ACR. Nothing is machine-translated. The Corporate workshop is the one exception, and it's explained below.</li>
    <li><strong>Contracting and payment.</strong> We contract as an independent foreign vendor, and we complete a Form W-8BEN for your finance team before the first payment. We invoice in US dollars, and we accept bank transfer or PayPal.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="prose">
  <h2>Express audit</h2>
  <p>A fast read on the state of your site, before you commit budget to a bigger project. We review the home page at minimum, plus the key pages that matter most on your site. It's a first pass: it tells you where you stand, and it doesn't replace the page-by-page report. Delivered in 2 to 3 business days, paid in full up front.</p>
  <details>
    <summary>What the express audit includes</summary>
    <div>
      <ul>
        <li>A manual review of the home page and a selection of key links.</li>
        <li>Direct observations, ordered by what has the most impact.</li>
        <li>A concrete recommendation on where to go next.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>What it doesn't include:</strong> the page-by-page report or the full criteria table. That's what the Site audit adds.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <p><a href="/en/contact/">Get a quote for an express audit</a></p>

  <h2>Site audit</h2>
  <p>The foundation for any accessibility statement that will hold up. <strong>We review the site page by page and link by link. We don't sample.</strong> The usual practice in this industry is a representative sample, around 8 to 15 pages. The W3C evaluation methodology treats sampling as the fallback and recommends evaluating the entire product wherever that's feasible. We make it feasible, against Level AA at minimum.</p>
  <details>
    <summary>What the site audit includes</summary>
    <div>
      <ul>
        <li>Evaluation page by page and link by link, not a sample.</li>
        <li>Level AA as the minimum, with AAA suggested wherever it's achievable.</li>
        <li>A full report with criteria table, screenshots, and examples.</li>
        <li>Both halves of the audit: the functional side, with keyboard and screen reader, and the visual side.</li>
        <li>A wrap-up call to walk through the findings. Bring whoever on your team needs to hear it.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>How it differs from the Express audit:</strong> the Express audit tells you whether you have a problem. This one tells you which problems, where they are, and what to fix first, in a document that stands up as the basis for an accessibility statement.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
  <p><a href="/en/contact/">Get a quote for a site audit</a></p>

