<div class="prose">
  <h2>ACR and VPAT, for selling into the United States</h2>
  <p>If a procurement team, a federal agency or an enterprise buyer has asked you for a VPAT, what they need is an <strong>ACR</strong>: an Accessibility Conformance Report, which is the VPAT template completed for your product, criterion by criterion, marked supports, partially supports, does not support or not applicable, with remarks. The VPAT is the blank form. The ACR is the document. Vendors report it under their own responsibility, which is exactly why a buyer reads it looking for who did the evaluation and how.</p>
  <p>That's where the scope of the audit underneath stops being a detail. An ACR backed by a sample of ten pages describes those ten pages. Ours is backed by a review of every page, so the remarks column describes your product and not an extract of it.</p>
  <p>We produce the edition your buyer asked for: WCAG, Section 508, EN 301 549 or the international edition.</p>

  <p><strong>Available on their own.</strong> The accessibility statement and the ACR can be ordered separately, based on an audit you already have. If someone is asking you for a certificate, read <a href="/en/web-accessibility-certificate/">which web accessibility certificates actually exist</a> and which document you need for the markets you sell into, before you pay for anything.</p>
</div>

<div class="prose">
  <h2>How we audit web accessibility</h2>
  <p>We combine automated tools with manual review using real screen readers: NVDA, JAWS, Windows Narrator, and TalkBack on Android. Automated tools cover the first pass. Manual testing is what finds the problems that matter, and there are no shortcuts there.</p>
  <p>We work from WCAG 2.2 as the common baseline, which covers WCAG 2.1 as well, so an audit against 2.2 answers a US court or a procurement team that asked for 2.1. From there we evaluate against whichever framework applies to you: EN 301 549 and the European Accessibility Act, the EAA, in Europe; ADA and Section 508 in the United States; or the relevant national regulation in Latin America. If you're not sure which one covers you, we explain it in <a href="/en/regulations/">which accessibility law applies to you</a>.</p>
  <p>Websites aren't all we audit. We also cover Android apps, PDF and EPUB documents, desktop software, and browser extensions.</p>
  <p>Every report is written for whoever has to read it. You can have it in any of these formats:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>A full document, with criteria table, screenshots, and examples.</li>
    <li>A spreadsheet, if you need the complete technical detail.</li>
    <li>A plain-language version, for the rest of the team.</li>
    <li>An ACR, the VPAT template filled in criterion by criterion, for selling into the United States. It can go on its own or alongside the full report.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>We don't stop at flagging the problem: we propose the fix.</p>
</div>

<div class="prose">
  <h2>Working terms</h2>
  <p>We work with direct clients and with agencies, and we do our best work direct. On the Site audit, the Full package and the Corporate workshop, 50% is due when the contract is signed and the rest on delivery. The Express audit is the exception: it's small and fast, so it's paid in full up front.</p>
  <p>We accept PayPal and bank transfer from Europe, Argentina, or the US. We quote in euros for Europe and dollars for the Americas.</p>
  <p><strong>Contract.</strong> Once we agree on the work, we send our own contract: scope, deliverables and timelines in writing, not just a quote. Auditing a product means seeing things that aren't public yet, so if your company needs an NDA, we'll sign yours.</p>
  <p><strong>Scope.</strong> We deliver the diagnosis, the prioritization, and the recommendations. Implementation, and the product's conformance, stay with whoever builds it. The exception is the Full package, which includes support through that implementation.</p>
</div>

